# A Heroes function handed to C, and C calling it back (§4.19; panel 111 R4,
# M-c-callbacks). Before this, `atexit`, `qsort`, `signal`, `sqlite3_exec` and
# every raylib callback were unbindable by two different doors: declared `ptr`
# the declaration was accepted and the CALL SITE failed `type_mismatch`;
# declared at its real type the DECLARATION failed `ffi_type`.
#
# What makes it cost nothing in the backend: a Heroes `function bye()` is
# emitted as `void h_x_bye(void)`, which IS what `void (*)(void)` points at, so
# the address goes to C with no cast and no trampoline. Panel 030 R6 had
# deferred this on the worry that a C11 backend could not express it; measured,
# it always could.
#
# AND IT IS ALSO THE GUARD'S QUIET DIRECTION. `bye` carries a
# `hero_thread_guard` call at its entry, because this program hands C a function
# (panel 111 R9). `atexit` runs its handlers on the thread that called `exit`,
# which is this program's own, so the guard passes and says nothing — which is
# the half a case can easily forget to assert. The loud direction needs a thread
# C made, and `pthread.h` is not on all three platforms, so it is asserted in
# the compiler's own tests instead of here.

extern "stdlib.h"
    function atexit(f: (function() -> ())) -> i32

function bye()
    print("bye")

function main()
    print("before")

    # The result is USED rather than discarded: `atexit` answers 0 on success,
    # and a binding whose result nothing reads is a different case from this one.
    if atexit(bye) != 0
        print("atexit refused the handler")
